From 7ec21c311024a3ba6d5ff3c50d5b4e19a03ef114 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yasuhiro Niji <aiseed-dev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 00:43:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] [se] Convert Japanese text in ASC and JIS, and count bytes in
 the B functions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ASC added 0x20 to every code from U+FF00 to U+FFEF and kept the low
byte, so half-width katakana became control characters and full-width
katakana stayed as they were. JIS subtracted 0x20 from every other
code, which turned hiragana and kanji into unrelated characters. Both
now follow ECMA-376 Part 1, 18.17.7.11 and 18.17.7.187, whose examples
are ASC("エクセル") = ｴｸｾﾙ and JIS("ｴｸｾﾙ") = エクセル: full-width ASCII,
the ideographic space and katakana become half-width and back, a voiced
kana is one full-width character or two half-width ones (ガ, ｶﾞ), and
hiragana and kanji are left alone. As in code page 932, ￥ and \ are the
same character. JIS is no longer hidden from the function list.

LENB, LEFTB, RIGHTB, MIDB, REPLACEB, FINDB and SEARCHB counted
characters, so LENB("あいう") was 3 where a Japanese spreadsheet has 6.
They now count the bytes of code page 932 (18.17.7.192, 18.17.7.194):
ASCII and half-width katakana take one byte, other characters two. Half
of a two-byte character at a cut becomes a space, as LibreOffice does.

VALUE reads full-width digits and signs (VALUE("１２３") = 123).

Five expectations in the ASC tests recorded the old output, such as
ASC("デジタル") = デジタル and a control character for U+FFEF; they now
expect the converted text.

Assisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5
